{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:8ed0ad9a7d2a494024cf863a1782d0bda76c2045dea198fe5898e8dc34791131","modelForecastIds":["sha256:9e32442dd80104ef31ec692efe35319d2d9fb5d07be418ab3e540ee18caa519a"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.7},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.5},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.5}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PPP WEIGHT RECONCILIATION (explicit, as required). OECD-38 PPP GDP ~$72T and China ~$41T in 2025 (combined ~$113T), so China's share is ~0.36; with China compounding ~3.5%/yr vs OECD ~1.6%/yr to 2031 the share drifts to ~0.385. I use w_China = 0.38, w_OECD = 0.62 for 2032-36. The adapter's own weights (0.629 / 0.371 at 2031) agree to within 1 pp despite a different regional partition, so the weighting is not where the model and I diverge. CHINA LEG AND THE -2.7 pp ARITHMETIC. w_China x locked China aging drag = 0.38 x (-2.7) = -1.03 pp of coupled growth. That single term is larger in absolute value than every AI channel in this payload combined, and it is the answer's dominant arithmetic. Bottom-up China 2032-36: labour -0.63 pp (working-age 20-64 contracting ~1.15%/yr as the 1962-68 cohorts cross 65 against small replacement cohorts; population -60M over 2026-35 and worker/retiree 4.4->2.8 per 2A; labour share 0.55 x -1.15); capital deepening +2.00 pp (K/Y ~3.2, I/Y falling from ~40% as residential investment stays ~30% below its 2021 peak, K growth decelerating to 4.5-5.0%/yr, alpha ~0.45); TFP +1.10 pp (~0.7 baseline plus ~0.4 AI); property/credit/pension-claim drag -0.50 pp (the 2.9T RMB / 10.1%-of-expenditure subsidy is a direct claim on fiscal capacity for productive complements). Sum = +1.97%/yr. CROSS-CHECK: a frictionless China counterfactual of ~4.7%/yr less the locked -2.7 pp = 2.0%. The two routes agree, so I adopt China = 2.0%/yr. OECD-38 LEG. Hours -0.05%/yr (headcount -0.10, participation +0.25 from the locked healthy-aging capacity gain, hours/worker -0.20). Labour quality/composition +0.15 pp. Baseline capital deepening +0.35 pp. INCREMENTAL AI/GRID CAPITAL DEEPENING +0.06 pp — and this is the quietest important number here: AI-Q3 P50 implies a 635 TWh increment over 2024, which at 70% IT utilization is ~104 GW of IT load, at $30-35M/MW all-in is ~$3.2-3.6T cumulative, ~$270-300B/yr net plus a 3-5 yr GPU replacement cycle giving ~$400-500B/yr gross, plus ~$250B/yr of grid, i.e. ~0.55 pp of extra I/Y on a ~$130T PPP base; at K/Y = 3.0 that lifts K growth by 0.55/3.0 = 0.18%/yr and at alpha = 0.35 contributes only +0.06 pp/yr to GDP growth. A capex boom enormous in dollars is near-trivial in growth-accounting terms because the existing capital stock is vast, alpha is a third, and short GPU service lives mean most gross spend is replacement rather than deepening. TFP +0.60 pp = 0.30 baseline (2015-22) plus +0.304 AI, where the AI term is taken from AI-Q6's own regime mix: 0.162 x 0.70 + 0.472 x 0.35 + 0.365 x 0.07 = +0.304 pp. OECD pre-coupling = 1.11%/yr. COUPLING ADJUSTMENTS (both directions, as the brief demands): -0.12 fiscal consolidation (~1% of GDP over the window at a 0.6 multiplier, forced by the 2%->3% net interest claim); -0.02 higher hurdle rate on long-duration grid/datacenter projects; -0.03 Baumol composition (moving 3 pp of hours from a 1.0%-productivity-growth economy into a ~0.15% care sector: 0.03 x 0.85); +0.05 automation demand-pull from labour scarcity. OECD coupled = 0.99%/yr. INDEPENDENT CHECK AGAINST 2A: the locked prior gives OECD per-capita growth at 0.6%/yr absent productivity offsets; with OECD-38 population +0.15%/yr that is 0.75%/yr aggregate, plus my +0.30 pp AI offset = 1.05%/yr. My bottom-up 0.99% reproduces the locked marginal to within 6 bp, so the OECD leg is 2A-consistent by construction. ASSEMBLY: 0.62 x 0.99 + 0.38 x 1.97 = 0.61 + 0.75 = 1.37%/yr; I round P50 to 1.5%/yr to allow for the capital-deepening term being my least-anchored estimate on both legs. INTERVAL: P10 0.7 (coupled AI-capex bust resolving AI-Q4 in 2027-32 leaving 2032-36 as the digestion window, -0.4 pp, plus a China property/pension accident, -0.4 pp); P90 2.5 (AI-Q6 Transformative Breakout at 16.2% mass adding +0.4 pp to the coupled TFP term, plus China capital deepening at the top of its range). MODEL DISAGREEMENT (rule 3 — the payload). The adapter returns coupled 2032-36 growth of 2.95% (baseline), 2.95% (central-path), 3.03% (ai-energy-boom), 2.30% (tech-stagnation), 2.29% (tech-plateau), 1.67% (debt-populism), 2.62% (ssp3-70), 3.68% (tech-breakthrough), 3.90% (net-zero). My 1.5% sits BELOW the model's entire ordinary range and only debt-populism (1.67%) comes near it. THE EXPLANATION IS STRUCTURAL, NOT CALIBRATIONAL, and it has a clean internal diagnostic. (a) The model's production function is Ayres-Warr with gamma = 0.55 on useful energy — CLAUDE.md documents this as a deliberate heterodox choice against a mainstream cost-share gamma of 0.05-0.08 — so growth there is overwhelmingly a useful-energy-and-efficiency story in which labour enters only through beta. The documented dominant GDP dial is serviceEfficiencyGrowth (~1.8x per plausible range), i.e. a second-law efficiency parameter, not a demographic outcome. (b) THE DIAGNOSTIC: the adapter discloses the model's demographic path tracks the UN WPP LOW variant (~18% below medium by 2100), which should bias its labour force and growth DOWN relative to 2A's baseline. That it nonetheless prints 2.95% tells you aging is nearly irrelevant to this model by construction — a low-fertility labour path coexisting with ~3% growth is proof that the energy/efficiency channel is swamping the labour channel, not evidence that growth will be high. Every bound I read off it is therefore LOW-FERTILITY-CONDITIONAL and simultaneously labour-insensitive. (c) The model has no care sector, no ISIC split, no Baumol composition term, no licensing constraint, no fiscal crowding-out of public complements, and no China pension-subsidy claim — i.e. none of the five mechanisms that produce my -1.03 pp China term and my -0.17 pp of OECD coupling drag. (d) China at 3.81%/yr (2.85% in tech-stagnation, 5.29% in net-zero) simply does not implement the locked -2.7 pp/yr aging drag; its 3-cohort fertility-convergence demographics feed a high-alpha capital-deepening engine with no property deleveraging and no fiscal claim. (e) Datacenter load: baseline world 1,884 TWh in 2036 vs the locked AI-Q3 P90 of 1,574 TWh for OECD-38+China. Since world is a SUPERSET, 1,884 world is roughly 1,200-1,300 for OECD+China at a 60-70% share, i.e. comfortably INSIDE the locked distribution and not actually a contradiction in baseline. The real conflict is ai-energy-boom at 4,180 TWh world (~2,700 OECD+China), far above the locked P90 — which identifies what the locked AI-Q3 prior implicitly assumes: a willingness-to-pay ceiling on the electricity-bill share of GDP. Fermi check of that equivalence: 1,573.8 TWh x $80/MWh = $126B/yr against ~$130T PPP = 0.097% of GDP, so AI-Q3's P90 is approximately a 0.10%-of-GDP power-bill ceiling, about 2x the model's own 0.05% default. The locked prior and the model agree on the MECHANISM (price-capped load) and differ only on the ceiling's level. (f) USABLE CONDITIONAL BOUND I DO TAKE from the model, with its translation published: robotsPer1000 (world, all sectors) reaches 3.92 in baseline, 6.09 in automation-boom, 6.63 in ai-energy-boom by 2036; divided by 1000 that is 0.39-0.66% of workers' worth of installed robot stock, and physical care robots are a small subset of that. TRANSLATION: this bounds the \"robots fill the elder-care gap\" channel at under ~0.7% of the labour force by 2036 even under the model's most aggressive automation scenario, against a care need of 18.3M workers (3.0% of OECD-38 employment). That channel is arithmetically closed inside the resolution horizon — low-fertility-conditional, and conditional on the model's endogenous marginal-value-versus-marginal-cost deployment rule rather than on any hard saturation cap. I adopt the model's BOUND on robots and reject its growth level.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.358Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD Health at a Glance and national LTC workforce statistics show OECD-38 health-and-social-work (ISIC Q) employment growing >=2.5%/yr over 2026-2029 (versus the ~1.5-1.8%/yr my rationing view implies) WITH reported LTC vacancy rates falling below 8% in at least five of the G7 plus Korea.\",\"by_when\":\"Mid-2030 (on 2029 reference-year data).\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central claim — that the care bottleneck clears by quantity rationing rather than by price, and that the J/K/M-to-Q conversion rate is ~7% of the needed flow — is wrong. The queue is clearing, so hypothesis plank (a) is far better than 7 (move it to 35-45). CQ1 rises 6-9 pp (to ~18-20, since both conjuncts benefit: a clearing care sector absorbs displaced workers AND bids wages). Move 5-8 points from CQ6-C to CQ6-B and A, since the supply wall is the main C generator. Raise CQ3 P50 by about +0.15 pp to ~1.65%, via a smaller Baumol composition drag and fewer family caregivers withdrawing from the labour force.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS and Eurostat sectoral median hourly earnings series show real median pay in ISIC F + Q outpacing ISIC J + K + M by >=4.5 percentage points cumulatively over 2025-2030 — i.e. on pace for the +10 pp the CQ1 conjunct needs by 2036 — with the gap widening rather than flat.\",\"by_when\":\"Q3 2031 (2030 reference-year releases).\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My conjunct-B marginal of 0.18 was too low, chiefly because 